corrective_actions:
In summary, the lessons learned from this event reinforce the need to:
• Expand the consideration of human factors in all phases of the operational cycle;
• Improve communication and reporting mechanisms, encouraging the active participation of workers;
Safety performance indicators – Process safety events – 2025 data
109

===PAGE_110===
• Promote periodic reviews of norms and standards, ensuring they are up-to-date, clear, and aligned with the real 
operational context;
• Strengthen training, simulations, and behavioural verification practices to reduce uncertainties and variations in 
the execution of critical activities.

what_went_wrong:
1. PTW procedure does not establish appropriate approvers and co-approvers; 
2. Instructions to workers not adequate; The PTW form does not reference the document that should be consulted 
to assist in identifying the equipment as critical; 
3. Unspecified simultaneous operations (SIMOP); The PTW procedure does not establish that open PTWs and 
ongoing operations are evaluated for simultaneity before releasing new PTWs; 
4. Lack of operational procedure for ventilation of gases through the lances; 
5. The well’s WSOG (Well Specific Operation Guidelines) provided for the existence of gas parameters for changing 
the DP status, being: “advisory” - any gas alarm; and yellow alarm - “confirmed gas”, there being no objective 
definition of the concept of “confirmed gas”.

corrective_actions:
1. Review PTW procedure including having the system/equipment end user leader co-approve for work release and 
train the workforce after review; 
2. Train the workforce on the criticality classification of the equipment or system contained in the Service Order 
linked to the task; 
3. Review procedure of “Work Permit System” including clear guidance for assessment of concurrency and approval 
flow for work permits that were not discussed in the previous day’s planning meeting; 
4. Include in the risk analysis of the service company’s task of operating the Workover Plant the step of checking the 
alignments and integrity of the manifold and boom lines connected to the plant; 
5. Insert a specific line for gas in the WSOG with objective-numeric criteria for “advisory” and yellow alarm.
